Signage and graphics") ]

Polyvinyl chloride is formed in flat sheets in a variety of thicknesses and
colors. As flat sheets, PVC is often expanded to create voids in the interior
of the material, providing additional thickness without additional weight and
minimal extra cost (see [ closed-cell PVC foamboard ](/wiki/Closed-
cell_PVC_foamboard "Closed-cell PVC foamboard") ). Sheets are cut using saws
and rotary cutting equipment.

Plasticized PVC is also used to produce thin, colored, or clear, [ adhesive
](/wiki/Adhesive "Adhesive") -backed films referred to simply as "vinyl".
These films are typically cut on a [ computer ](/wiki/Computer "Computer")
-controlled [ plotter ](/wiki/Plotter "Plotter") (see [ vinyl cutter
](/wiki/Vinyl_cutter "Vinyl cutter") ) or printed in a [ wide-format printer
](/wiki/Wide-format_printer "Wide-format printer") . These sheets and films
are used to produce a wide variety of [ commercial signage
](/wiki/Commercial_signage "Commercial signage") products, [ vinyl wraps
](/wiki/Wrap_advertising "Wrap advertising") or [ racing stripes
](/wiki/Racing_stripe "Racing stripe") on vehicles for aesthetics or as [ wrap
advertising ](/wiki/Wrap_advertising "Wrap advertising") , and general purpose
[ stickers ](/wiki/Sticker "Sticker") .  [41]

